      {
        "id": "s3_derive",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "The derivation",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Derivation",
        "lines": [
          "Let the particle move from P₁ to P₂",
          "in time Δt, covering arc Δs while the",
          "radius turns through Δθ. Since",
          "angle = arc / radius,",
          {
            "text": "Δs = r·Δθ",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "Dividing both sides by Δt,",
          {
            "text": "Δs/Δt = r · Δθ/Δt",
            "style": "eq"
          }
        ],
        "margin_note": "Two moves only: the arc-angle relation, then divide by Δt. Name both as you write them.",
        "why": "Everything comes from the definition of the radian: an angle IS arc over radius. Divide by time and the two rates appear.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Starting from v = rω itself — that is the thing being derived."
        ],
        "recall": {
          "credit": "say_it",
          "must_convey": "That arc = radius × angle gives Δs = rΔθ, then dividing by Δt relates the two rates.",
          "accept": [
            "arc equals radius into angle",
            "delta s is r delta theta",
            "divide by delta t"
          ],
          "reject": [
            "omega is d theta by d t"
          ],
          "heard_as": [
            "arc",
            "r delta theta",
            "divide"
          ]
        },
        "memory_tip": "The radian definition does everything: Δθ = Δs/r. Divide both sides by Δt."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_result",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "The result",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Result",
        "lines": [
          "In the limit Δt → 0, Δs/Δt = v and",
          "Δθ/Δt = ω, so",
          {
            "text": "v = r ω",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "Linear velocity = radius × angular",
          "velocity."
        ],
        "margin_note": "Say the limit line — that is what turns the ratios into v and ω.",
